#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Backend for the Samsung TV remote bar widget.

The TV (a Tizen set with TokenAuthSupport) takes remote keys over a WebSocket
on port 8002 -- TLS with a self-signed cert, plain RFC6455 frames, short JSON
text messages. That is small enough to speak with the stdlib alone, which is
why there is no websocket-client dependency here: the widget must work on a
stock Arch/Omarchy box with nothing pip-installed.

Two modes:

  tvctl <key>...   one-shot; connects, sends, exits. Handy from a keybinding.
  tvctl serve      reads key names on stdin, one per line, and holds the
                   socket open. The panel uses this so a keypress costs one
                   small frame instead of a fresh TCP+TLS handshake.

Volume and mute are NOT available on the remote channel -- it only sends key
clicks, and the TV never reports back what the volume became. Those are read
separately over UPnP RenderingControl on port 9197, which is what lets the
panel show a real number instead of a guess.
"""
import base64
import hashlib
import json
import os
import re
import socket
import ssl
import struct
import sys
import threading
import time
import urllib.request

